::MCR_U05_L03_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L03_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.4
Question:
A number cube is rolled. What is the probability of rolling a 3 or a 4?
{
=A. 2/6#Correct. There are two favorable outcomes, 3 and 4, out of 6.
~B. 1/6#This counts only one of the two favorable outcomes.
~C. 2/12#This incorrectly multiplies for an or question.
~D. 4/6#This counts too many outcomes.
}
::MCR_U05_L03_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L03_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.4
Question:
A number cube is rolled. What is the probability of rolling an odd number or a number greater than 4?
{
~A. 5/6#This double-counts 5 because it is both odd and greater than 4.
=B. 4/6#Correct. The union is {1, 3, 5, 6}, or 4 out of 6.
~C. 2/6#This counts only numbers greater than 4.
~D. 3/6#This counts only odd numbers and leaves out 6.
}
::MCR_U05_L03_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L03_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.5
Question:
A coin is flipped and a number cube is rolled. What is the probability of tails and rolling a 2?
{
~A. 1/2#This only accounts for tails and ignores rolling a 2.
~B. 1/6#This only accounts for rolling a 2 and ignores tails.
=C. 1/12#Correct. Multiply 1/2 by 1/6 for the two independent events.
~D. 7/12#This adds probabilities instead of multiplying for and.
}
::MCR_U05_L03_Quiz_Q04::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L03_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.5
Question:
Two fair coins are flipped. What is the probability of getting two tails?
{
~A. 1/2#This is the probability of tails on one coin only.
~B. 2/4#This counts outcomes with exactly one tail, not two tails.
~C. 3/4#This counts too many outcomes.
=D. 1/4#Correct. Only TT out of HH, HT, TH, TT has two tails.
}
::MCR_U05_L03_Quiz_Q05::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L03_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.4
Question:
A number cube is rolled. What is the probability of rolling a number less than 3 or a 6?
{
=A. 3/6#Correct. The favorable outcomes are {1, 2, 6}.
~B. 2/6#This counts only numbers less than 3 and leaves out 6.
~C. 1/6#This counts only rolling 6.
~D. 3/12#This incorrectly multiplies for an or situation.
}
::MCR_U05_L03_Quiz_Q06::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L03_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.5
Question:
A spinner with 4 equal colors is spun and a coin is flipped. What is the probability of landing on blue and heads?
{
~A. 1/4#This accounts only for blue.
=B. 1/8#Correct. Multiply 1/4 by 1/2 to get 1/8.
~C. 1/2#This accounts only for heads.
~D. 3/4#This is not both events happening.
}
::MCR_U05_L03_Quiz_Q07::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L03_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.4
Question:
Which word usually signals a union in probability?
{
~A. and#And usually signals that both events must happen.
~B. given#Given is commonly used with conditional probability.
=C. or#Correct. Or means the outcome can be in one event, the other event, or both.
~D. after#After may signal order, not union.
}
::MCR_U05_L03_Quiz_Q08::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L03_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.5
Question:
Which word usually signals both events must happen?
{
~A. or#Or usually signals a union.
~B. not#Not usually signals a complement.
~C. either#Either usually connects to or.
=D. and#Correct. And means both event conditions must be satisfied.
}
::MCR_U05_L03_Quiz_Q09::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L03_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.4
Question:
A number cube is rolled. What is P(even or 1)?
{
=A. 4/6#Correct. The favorable outcomes are {1, 2, 4, 6}.
~B. 3/6#This counts only even outcomes and omits 1.
~C. 1/6#This counts only rolling 1.
~D. 3/12#This incorrectly multiplies for or.
}
::MCR_U05_L03_Quiz_Q10::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L03_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.5
Question:
A coin is flipped twice. What is the probability of heads first and tails second?
{
~A. 1/2#This only describes one flip.
=B. 1/4#Correct. The ordered outcomes are HH, HT, TH, TT, and only HT works.
~C. 2/4#This counts two outcomes with one head and one tail, but order matters here.
~D. 3/4#This counts too many outcomes.
}
::MCR_U05_L03_Quiz_Q11::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L03_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.4
Question:
A number cube is rolled. What is P(less than 4 or greater than 4)?
{
~A. 6/6#This includes 4, but 4 is neither less than 4 nor greater than 4.
~B. 3/6#This counts only less than 4.
=C. 5/6#Correct. The favorable outcomes are {1, 2, 3, 5, 6}.
~D. 1/6#This counts only the excluded value 4.
}
::MCR_U05_L03_Quiz_Q12::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L03_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.5
Question:
A card is chosen from {red, blue, green} and a coin is flipped. What is P(red and tails)?
{
~A. 1/3#This accounts only for red.
~B. 1/2#This accounts only for tails.
~C. 2/5#This adds unrelated counts.
=D. 1/6#Correct. Multiply 1/3 by 1/2.
}
::MCR_U05_L03_Quiz_Q13::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L03_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.4
Question:
Why subtract overlap in some or probability problems?
{
=A. The overlap was counted twice when both event probabilities were added.#Correct. Subtracting overlap keeps shared outcomes from being double-counted.
~B. The overlap should never be included.#Overlap is included once in the union.
~C. The sample space changes after adding.#The sample space does not change.
~D. Addition is never used for or questions.#Addition can be used, but overlap must be handled.
}
::MCR_U05_L03_Quiz_Q14::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L03_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.5
Question:
Events are independent when:
{
~A. they must be subtracted#Subtraction is not the meaning of independence.
=B. one event does not change the probability of the other#Correct. Independence means one result does not affect the chance of the other.
~C. they have no sample space#All probability events need a sample space.
~D. they always overlap#Overlapping is not the definition of independence.
}
::MCR_U05_L03_Quiz_Q15::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L03_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.4
Question:
A number cube is rolled. What is P(odd or less than 3)?
{
~A. 5/6#This double-counts 1 and counts too many outcomes.
~B. 2/6#This counts only less than 3.
=C. 4/6#Correct. The union is {1, 2, 3, 5}.
~D. 1/6#This counts only the overlap {1}.
}
::MCR_U05_L03_Quiz_Q16::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L03_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.5
Question:
A fair coin is flipped and a 4-section spinner is spun. What is P(tails and green)?
{
~A. 1/2#This accounts only for tails.
~B. 1/4#This accounts only for green.
~C. 3/4#This is not both events together.
=D. 1/8#Correct. Multiply 1/2 by 1/4.
}
::MCR_U05_L03_Quiz_Q17::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L03_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.4
Question:
A number cube is rolled. What is P(1 or even)?
{
=A. 4/6#Correct. The favorable outcomes are {1, 2, 4, 6}.
~B. 3/6#This leaves out 1.
~C. 1/6#This counts only 1.
~D. 2/6#This counts too few favorable outcomes.
}
::MCR_U05_L03_Quiz_Q18::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L03_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.5
Question:
Two independent events have probabilities 1/3 and 1/5. What is the probability that both happen?
{
~A. 2/8#This adds numerators and denominators, which is not the multiplication rule.
=B. 1/15#Correct. Multiply 1/3 by 1/5.
~C. 2/15#This adds the numerators instead of multiplying.
~D. 1/8#This adds denominators instead of multiplying.
}
::MCR_U05_L03_Quiz_Q19::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L03_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.4
Question:
A number cube is rolled. What is P(greater than 2 or less than 5)?
{
~A. 4/6#This counts only greater than 2 or only less than 5, not the union.
~B. 5/6#This misses that all numbers 1 through 6 are covered by one of the two descriptions.
=C. 6/6#Correct. Every outcome is either greater than 2 or less than 5.
~D. 2/6#This counts only the overlap.
}
::MCR_U05_L03_Quiz_Q20::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L03_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.5
Question:
What rule is most appropriate for independent events connected by and?
{
~A. Addition rule#Addition is generally used for or.
~B. Complement rule#Complement is used for not the event.
~C. Overlap subtraction only#Overlap subtraction belongs with some or questions.
=D. Multiplication rule#Correct. Independent events connected by and use multiplication.
}
::MCR_U05_L03_Quiz_Q21::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L03_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.4
Question:
A number cube is rolled. What is P(2 or 4 or 6)?
{
=A. 3/6#Correct. There are three favorable outcomes: 2, 4, and 6.
~B. 2/6#This counts only two favorable outcomes.
~C. 1/6#This counts only one favorable outcome.
~D. 6/3#This reverses the probability ratio.
}
::MCR_U05_L03_Quiz_Q22::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L03_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.5
Question:
A coin is flipped three times. What is the probability of heads on all three flips?
{
~A. 1/2#This is for one flip only.
=B. 1/8#Correct. Multiply 1/2 by 1/2 by 1/2.
~C. 3/6#This adds flip counts instead of multiplying probabilities.
~D. 3/8#This counts too many outcomes.
}
::MCR_U05_L03_Quiz_Q23::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L03_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.4
Question:
If A and B do not overlap, what is P(A or B)?
{
~A. P(A) x P(B)#That is used for some and situations, not or.
~B. P(A) - P(B)#Subtracting one event from another is not the union rule.
=C. P(A) + P(B)#Correct. If there is no overlap, add the probabilities.
~D. P(A) / P(B)#Division is not the addition rule.
}
::MCR_U05_L03_Quiz_Q24::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L03_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.4
Question:
If A and B overlap, what adjustment is needed when finding P(A or B)?
{
~A. Add the overlap twice#This makes the double-counting worse.
~B. Ignore both events#The events are still needed.
~C. Multiply the overlap by the total#This does not correct the union.
=D. Subtract the overlap once#Correct. Subtracting the overlap once prevents double-counting.
}
::MCR_U05_L03_Quiz_Q25::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L03_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.5
Question:
A student chooses one of 5 folders and one of 2 pens. What is the probability of choosing the blue folder and black pen if each choice is equally likely?
{
=A. 1/10#Correct. Multiply 1/5 by 1/2 because both independent choices must happen.
~B. 1/7#This adds the number of choices instead of multiplying.
~C. 2/5#This does not account for both choices.
~D. 5/2#This reverses part of the probability relationship.
}
